# Site markdown (`content/**/*.md`)

## Content placement

- MUST choose `content/<section>/` and `type` using **`.cursor/rules/content-placement.mdc`** before drafting or refactoring front matter.

## Language

- MUST write body copy, titles, `description`, and `grounding` in English only for **default-language** pages (no language suffix, or explicit `*.en.md` when used).
- MUST use **US English** spelling and vocabulary on default-language pages (largest global audience). Examples: **generalize**, **behavior**, **center**, **organize**. MUST NOT mix UK variants (**generalise**, **behaviour**, **centre**) in the same post unless quoting a source or title.
- For **Spanish** sibling pages (`*.es.md` next to the English source), MUST write those fields in Spanish and MUST follow **`.cursor/skills/spanish-translation-content/SKILL.md`** (translationKey, taxonomies, type-specific fields).
- MUST keep register informal-standard: plain vocabulary; use technical or academic terms only for named constructs, citations, or definitions.
- MUST default to short declarative sentences in **titles**, **`###` headings**, and **list bullets**. Use one longer sentence only for setup; do not chain many subclauses.
- For **claims / video / Substack body** (and matching sidecars): default is **2–4 sentence mechanism paragraphs** (see **Explanatory prose**). MUST NOT meet the short-sentence rule by stacking two punch lines that restack one thesis.
- MUST use repetition only for rhetorical emphasis, not padding.

## Publish `date`

- For posts that set a full timestamp, MUST use **`date: 'YYYY-MM-DDT01:00:00+11:00'`** (single-quoted RFC3339-style string, wall clock **01:00**, offset **`+11:00`**) unless the author documents a different convention for that file.
- MUST keep the **calendar day** aligned with the bundle folder name when the folder encodes the intended publish day (for example `2026-05-03-...` → `2026-05-03` in **`date`**).
- MUST NOT mix arbitrary offsets or times (`+10:00`, `T10:00:00`, double-quoted **`date`**) on new or updated posts without a stated reason.

## Publish calendar (after bundle create or reschedule)

- When an agent **creates** any post bundle under **`content/`**, **renames or moves** a bundle folder, or changes **`date`** or **`draft`** on any page, MUST run **`make calendar`** in the same session before marking the task done (alias: **`make publish-calendar`**).
- That refreshes **`static/calendar/publish-calendar.json`** (all sections and types; UI at **`/calendar/`** under **`make serve`**).
- MUST commit the updated JSON when the user asks for a commit that includes the content change.
- Skip only when the user says to skip calendar, or when a prior run in the same session already regenerated after the same bundle change.

## Punctuation

- MUST NOT use the em dash character (U+2014). Use comma, semicolon, colon, or parentheses.

## Hooks, headings, and cold-read

- MUST follow **`.cursor/skills/revise-hooks/SKILL.md`** for **`title`**, list-facing teasers (**`description`**, Claim, **`grounding`**, **`sowhat`**), and body section headings.
- List-facing copy decides **capture vs pass**; MUST **cold-read** those fields without the body (type-specific fields in **revise-hooks**).
- Body **`##`** and **`###`** headings MUST be **hooks** (tension, question, punch), not seminar labels only (`### The Takeaway`, `### Ideas that travel`). For **`type: video`** site body copy, use **`###`** (and **`### Chapter Guide`**) so headings sit under the video single’s **`h2`** subtitle and **`h3`** lead blocks; see **`.cursor/skills/video-content/SKILL.md`** → **Body headings**. Claims **Thoughts** use **`###`**; neutral nav headings on video use **`### Chapter Guide`**.

## Emphasis (Markdown bold)

- MUST follow **`.cursor/skills/revise-emphasis/SKILL.md`** whenever adding or editing **`**bold**`** in front matter or body under `content/` (all `type` values). Default is **restrained** emphasis; do not use wall-to-wall bold on cards or sections.

## Raster images in `content/` page bundles

- MUST follow **`.cursor/rules/content-images-webp.mdc`** when a bundle under **`content/`** includes **`.png`** rasters (convert to **`.webp`**, update references, verify build).

## Front matter when `type: claims`

- MUST treat `description` as the Claim: it must read clearly alone in list and card views.
- MUST treat `grounding` as support for the Claim: definitions, named constructs, dates, and links. It is not a full article and not a substitute for an optional long quotation in the body.
- MUST follow `.cursor/skills/claims-content/SKILL.md` for field roles and optional primary-source quotes: introduce them with a plain **`###`** heading (no leading emoji on **Thoughts** subsections), then the blockquote; do **not** use a plain `[WHAT HE SAID]` line.

## Optional leading emoji in `title` (`social-protocols`, `human-condition`, `mind-infrastructure`)

- For posts under **`content/social-protocols/`**, **`content/human-condition/`**, or **`content/mind-infrastructure/`**, MAY prefix **`title`** with **one or two** leading emoji when they **signal the hook** (tone or metaphor). Plain titles remain valid.
- MUST **not** use this pattern under **`content/cognitive-memetics/`**; there MUST **not** be leading emoji in **`title`** there (use optional **`heading_code`** when you need a prefix; see **`.cursor/skills/cognitive-memetics-content/SKILL.md`**).
- MUST NOT use **more than two** leading emoji in **`title`** where this pattern is allowed.
- MUST keep **`description`** without decorative emoji: it is the **Claim** for **`type: claims`** and the **lead** for **`type: video`**. MUST keep **`grounding`** (when present) without decorative emoji.

## Front matter under `content/cognitive-memetics/`

- MUST **not** prefix **`title`** with leading emoji; optional title emoji applies only in **`social-protocols`**, **`human-condition`**, and **`mind-infrastructure`** (see **Optional leading emoji in `title`** above).
- MUST follow `.cursor/skills/cognitive-memetics-content/SKILL.md` for **umbrella + project** `categories` (shareable `/categories/...` hubs: **Cube-Cows**, **Por-Estas-Calles**, **T-Shirt Art**), cube-cows vs sayings fields, and **Street Wisdom** tags when that hub applies (including LinkedIn hashtag mapping).
- MUST preserve the author’s existing **`title`**, **`tldr`**, **`fluff`**, and body prose unless they explicitly ask for a rewrite, revise, tighten, or similar. For hubs other than **Por-Estas-Calles**, MUST preserve a **shipped** **`description`** the same way until they ask to rewrite the teaser. MUST **ask first** before any other prose edits; see **Ask before changing existing copy** in `.cursor/skills/cognitive-memetics-content/SKILL.md`. For **Por-Estas-Calles**, applying that skill **requires** **Sayings emphasis** on **`tldr`** / **`fluff`** per **`.cursor/skills/revise-emphasis/SKILL.md`** and **Por-Estas-Calles card teaser** (replace **`description`** from **`title`**, **`tldr`**, **`fluff`** only). For every other Cognitive-Memetics hub, new or rewritten teasers MUST follow **Gemma teaser (`description`)** in that skill (local Gemma 4; not agent-only captions). Bold on existing words follows **revise-emphasis** and the relevant type skill.

## Front matter when `type: video`

- MUST treat `description` as the **lead** above the embed: it must read well alone in list and card views.
- MUST follow `.cursor/skills/video-content/SKILL.md` for **`youtube_id`** vs body shortcode, **`categories`** (topic hubs such as Mind-Infrastructure, Human-Condition, or Social-Protocols), section folder per **`.cursor/rules/content-placement.mdc`**, tags, optional featured image, **lead that invites a full watch**, and **TLDR body** (so-what article for text-first readers).

## Voice while drafting

Draft to **`.cursor/skills/revise-post/SKILL.md`** → **Step 2** (voice scrub) and **Step 5** (negation-first) on the **first pass**, on **all prose** (body, `description`, `sowhat`, `grounding`, sidecars). MUST NOT write slack prose expecting a later revision pass to fix voice.

- MUST use **active voice**: named subjects perform actions. MUST NOT use passive piles ("It was determined that...", "it was found that...") when the actor is known.
- MUST state ideas **directly**. MUST NOT use teacher/editor framing, bridge verbs, semicolon aphorisms, watch/read CTAs, **clever filler metaphors**, **Industry-verb shells** (`sells the blend`, `steer the rewrite`, `assembling it`, `next assembly`, `template can harden`, "name the box… and the box"), **caption pivots**, **telegraphic compression**, **parallel contrast stacks**, **construct-labeling meta**, **academic accessibility jargon**, **mechanism jargon without a scene**, filler hedges, meta-commentary, vague intensifiers, adverb stacks, or **AI rhetoric**. Full banned-pattern table: **revise-post** → **Step 2**.
- MUST prefer spoken verbs and room-level scenes over packaged thesis pairs. Prefer "lie to the room / buy your own story" over "Some people… Others… **Self-deception** names that move."
- MUST NOT use **negation-first** or **negation fluff** ("It is not X; it is Y", "Not A but B", "as X, not Y", trailing ", not a fuel shortage"). State the positive claim instead. **Exception:** evidence-scope qualifiers that narrow overclaim ("This does not mean every criticism is projection") are allowed; see **revise-post** → **Step 5**.
- MUST gloss **named constructs and coined labels** in the **same sentence** on first use (`fixed energy budget: a limited daily pool…`). Cold-read rule: **revise-post** → **Step 3**.
- Every sentence and paragraph MUST pass **standalone worth**: if it stood alone, would it be worth saying? It must add a new fact, example, mechanism, stakes, or turn not already in the title, list copy, or previous paragraph. A new metaphor or coined label for the same claim is not a turn. CUT/MERGE rules: **revise-post** → **Step 2**.
- For AI, neuroscience, or cognitive science topics, MAY read **`.cursor/skills/ai-for-general-audience/SKILL.md`** while drafting plain-language glosses.
- Before publish, MUST still run **`.cursor/skills/revise-post/SKILL.md`** (default mode **`rough`**) even when the draft followed this section.

### Explanatory prose (claims, video, Substack)

For **long prose** in **`type: claims`** (Thoughts body), **`type: video`** (body, `description`, `sowhat`), and **`substack.md` / `substack.es.md`**, write like a clear human explaining a difficult idea, not a sequence of revelations. Keep the site's visual restraint; do **not** copy aphoristic fragment stacks into essays.

- MUST state the claim before interpreting it.
- MUST use normal paragraphs of **2–4 sentences** as the default (not one-line fragments).
- MUST prefer concrete verbs (`people deny`, `the mind protects`, `anger can distort judgment`).
- MUST keep evidence qualifications where needed (`can`, `may`, `in some cases`).
- MUST treat readers as intelligent: explain the mechanism or evidence, then let it carry the point.
- MUST NOT use rhetorical noun fragments ("The mirror.", "The wound.", "The truth.").
- MUST NOT restack the same claim in altered poetic language.
- MUST NOT ship **Metaphor-shell restack**: after sentence N, N+1 fails if it only relabels N, restates it as a tautology, or restates its implication with a new metaphor pair. Full test: **`.cursor/skills/revise-post/reference.md`** → **Metaphor-shell restack**.
- MUST NOT ship **Industry-verb shells**: a clause fails even alone if its payload is `sells`, `steer`, `assembling` / `assembly`, `harden`, a fake scene (`the same room`), or "name the box… and the box." Full row: **`.cursor/skills/revise-post/reference.md`** → **Industry-verb shells**.
- MUST use metaphors **rarely**, and only when they clarify.
- Aim for psychologically careful, evidence-led, morally restrained tone: no accusing the reader, no promising hidden insight.
- Full banned patterns, do/don't, and checks: **revise-post** → **Step 2** → **Explanatory prose**.
- **`cognitive-memetics`** (panel / sayings): use **Hybrid prose** in **`.cursor/skills/cognitive-memetics-content/SKILL.md`** (emotion + claim; ban empty punch closers). Not full essay Explanatory prose.

## Body

- MAY use second person for stakes or direct address. MUST NOT turn most paragraphs into imperative lists or manuals.
- MUST prefer concrete verbs over stacks of abstract nouns (see **Voice while drafting**). For claims, video, and Substack, metaphors are rare clarifiers, not the default texture.
- MUST use **bold** sparingly per **`.cursor/skills/revise-emphasis/SKILL.md`**, only for emphasis.
- MAY use emoji as section or title markers in non-claims content. For **`type: claims`**, do **not** use leading emoji on **`###`** headings under **Thoughts** (see `.cursor/skills/claims-content/SKILL.md`); leading emoji in **`title`** is optional only under **`content/social-protocols/`**, **`content/human-condition/`**, and **`content/mind-infrastructure/`**, and MUST **not** appear on **`title`** under **`content/cognitive-memetics/`** (see **Optional leading emoji in `title`** above). MUST NOT put emoji in most sentences.

## Do not

- MUST NOT add filler, vague intensifiers, stock openers (for example "as we all know"), or voice patterns listed under **Voice while drafting**.
- MUST NOT open by copying the Claim verbatim unless that repetition is intentional.
